{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§9 and 10 distinguish repeatability evidence from score interpretation. One score establishes neither stability nor instability; execution evidence is insufficient to rate precision (0), rather than evidence of inadequate precision (1).",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The material does not establish whether repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "A single reported score cannot demonstrate variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9, the execution record establishes that repeatability was not studied. Precision remains unrateable from these findings (0); absence of repetition does not demonstrate instability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The record explicitly excludes repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "Without repeated scores, instability is not demonstrated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_review": "Reinterpreting EFPA §9 repeatability for a fixed AI, six controlled evaluations meet the report's local stability rule: the range is two percentage points. This does not independently establish an EFPA precision rating or broader validity.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes repeated evaluations under matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The two-point range is within the stated three-point maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §9, the controlled repetitions provide direct evidence of score variation. Their 31-point range fails the stated three-point stability rule; this is an observed limitation, not merely missing evidence.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "with identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The same AI and problems were evaluated repeatedly with matching conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The 31-point range exceeds the stated three-point maximum."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6 separates documentation from demonstrated technical quality. Topics and marking instructions do not establish answer accuracy (§§6.1 and 10.1) or repeatability (§9); both evidence gaps remain unrateable rather than proven deficiencies.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "Repeated identical-condition evaluations are not documented."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "The material supplies no evidence demonstrating answer mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_review": "The appendix adds procedural support relevant to EFPA §6.3.8. It supplies no additional answer-quality or precision evidence, so the gaps under §§6.1, 9 and 10.1 remain.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results or examination of answers.",
          "reason": "Installation information does not establish repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "Neither the original document nor the appendix demonstrates mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1 and 10.1 recognize the independent editorial examination as development evidence. Examination alone does not establish released-answer correctness. Reconciled notes without initial agreement counts also do not establish an agreement coefficient; editorial agreement is distinct from response-scoring reliability (§9.6).",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "Both components underwent independent editorial checking during development."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "The review process does not establish that every released answer is correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1 and 10.1 distinguish development procedures from their outcomes: independent checking occurred, but twelve released answers are explicitly wrong. This is a demonstrated content limitation. Initial editorial agreement remains undocumented.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "The passage documents checking of both questions and answers."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "Twelve identified errors directly refute universal correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §6.3.2 requires evidence that automated scoring works correctly. Deterministic marks demonstrate consistency for identical text, not correctness. Under §9.6, inter-rater reliability is inapplicable to the described fixed, nonjudgmental software scoring.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported.",
          "reason": "An unreported comparison cannot be assumed either performed or absent."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "Consistent scoring does not establish correct scoring for every response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §6.3.2, independent checks provide direct scoring evidence, including two unresolved errors. Determinism does not remedy these discrepancies. The 20 chosen responses do not establish coverage of all possible responses.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The stated independent comparison was performed."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved.",
          "reason": "Observed disagreements with established correct marks refute universal scoring correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10 evaluates the intended interpretation: the counts support this explicitly bounded comparison. The ranking-only exception in §8.3.2.2 permits norms to be inapplicable for this sole purpose. Repeatability and broader generalization remain separate questions.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported counts directly answer the stated, evaluation-specific question."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "A solved twelve more evaluated problems under the reported conditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §10 requires validity evidence appropriate to the breadth of the intended use. These grid results establish the observed ordering but leave general intelligence across subjects and settings unestablished. Limited evidence is not proof that the broader ordering is false.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not justify the stated generalization across subjects and real-world settings."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "The observed grid-problem counts establish A's advantage in this evaluation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §§6.1.3.1, 6.3.2 and 10.1 assess scoring against its measurement aim. Binary scoring matches the explicit complete-grid requirement. Partial credit is not required here; scoring adequacy does not establish other technical qualities.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "One point for complete correctness and zero otherwise implements the stated requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Zero for partial correctness follows the requirement rather than violating it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA §§6.1.3.1, 6.3.2 and 10.1, scoring conflicts with its stated measurement aim. Assigning zero to partially correct grids discards credit explicitly required for correctly completed cells; this is demonstrated misalignment.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "give a response credit for every correctly completed cell",
          "reason": "The all-or-nothing rule withholds required cell-level credit."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "including partially correct output grids",
          "reason": "The requirement explicitly includes credit that the scoring rule denies."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_review": "EFPA §9 distinguishes identical-condition repeatability from sensitivity to altered administration. Reinterpreted for AI instructions, this design studies instruction changes, relevant to §§9.4 or 9.7 and potentially §10.3.1.6. It does not establish identical-instruction stability or alternate-form equivalence.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No evaluation repeats an identical instruction.",
          "reason": "Instructions change, so the evaluation conditions are not identical."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes while varying instructions and holding the listed other conditions fixed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_review": "Reinterpreting EFPA §9 for repeated AI evaluations, the design studies identical-condition score changes. It does not study instruction sensitivity. Without numerical results or precision estimates, the magnitude and adequacy of repeatability remain unrateable.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "software, resources, problem order, settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported score changes concern repeated evaluations under identical conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied.",
          "reason": "The design explicitly excludes instruction changes."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "Applied the supplied original EFPA 2025 model across Parts A–C, focusing each explanation on relevant provisions as permitted. Reinterpretations: AI systems replace human test takers; execution protocols replace administration conditions; benchmark scores and summaries replace test scores and reports. Human sensory, motor, demographic, clinical, credential and debriefing questions are inapplicable literally; relevant AI interface and group-comparability analogues require explicit evidence. Unreported factual, classification, supply, material, norm, precision, validity, fairness and digital-report details remain unknown, not automatically inapplicable or inadequate. Sections 4 and 12 require report-specific evidence; headline scores alone do not supply it. Original ratings distinguish 0 (insufficient information), 1 (inadequate), 2 (adequate), 3 (good), 4 (excellent) and n/a; claim labels are separate. Critical 0/1 attributes trigger EFPA's exceptional expert/research-use restriction for the stated purpose, without proving poor intrinsic quality. No aggregate quality score was calculated. Part C source: supplied EFPA (2025) extraction, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0; no external references consulted and no EFPA endorsement implied. No task-blocking instructions were unclear. The extraction's 7.2.8 summary reference conflicts with its listed 7.2.7 overall item; several technical anchors also contain apparent textual inconsistencies, none needed to decide these claims."
}
